{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 399,
    "dDurationMs": 4011,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Physicists have been hunting for one particle longer than perhaps any other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4410,
    "dDurationMs": 3950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s not the tachyon or some supersymmetric particle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8360,
    "dDurationMs": 5009,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s the magnetic monopole - and of all\nthe fantastical beasts of particle physics,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 13369,
    "dDurationMs": 4051,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this is perhaps the most likely to actually exist."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 17420,
    "dDurationMs": 1861,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So where are they all?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24469,
    "dDurationMs": 2123,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s try an experiment."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 26592,
    "dDurationMs": 3408,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Take a metal bar and force all the electrons\nto one end."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30000,
    "dDurationMs": 5330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The electric field of the bar now looks like\nthis - that’s a dipole field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 35330,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now cut the bar in half and you get a pair\nof electric charges - one negative and one"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 41270,
    "dDurationMs": 4790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "positive, both of which have electric fields\nthat radiate straight out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46060,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now take a metal bar and intsead magnetize it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50420,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You get a dipole magnetic field that’s very\nsimilar to the dipole electric field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55020,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So if we cut this bar in half surely we get\na pair of magnetic charges similar to our"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60840,
    "dDurationMs": 2550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electric charges, right?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63390,
    "dDurationMs": 1080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Wrong."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 64470,
    "dDurationMs": 6170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The ends of the split magnet still have north\nand south poles, and still generate a dipole"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70640,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71640,
    "dDurationMs": 4100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And according to classical electromagnetism, it doesn’t matter how many times you slice"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75740,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it - you’ll never get isolated magnetic\ncharges - what we call magnetic monopoles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81580,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This magnet-slicing experiment was first performed by French scholar and Beauxbatons Academy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86900,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Professor Petrus Peregrinus de Marincourt\nway back in 1269."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91580,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That was before we knew what caused the magnetism in magnets."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96460,
    "dDurationMs": 5199,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These days we know where magnetism comes from we’re not so surprised that a halved magnet"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101659,
    "dDurationMs": 2121,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just makes two smaller magnets."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103780,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In a ferromagnet, the field is the sum of\nthe countless tiny aligned dipole fields of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 109420,
    "dDurationMs": 2370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electrons in the magnet’s atoms."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 111790,
    "dDurationMs": 5329,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The other popular way to make a dipole magnetic field is the electromagnet - where were push"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 117119,
    "dDurationMs": 1771,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electrons around in a circle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118890,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In both cases - electron spin or or a circular electric current there’s a sense of electric"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124930,
    "dDurationMs": 1090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "charge in motion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 126020,
    "dDurationMs": 4919,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And according to classical electrodynamics, moving electric charge is the source of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130939,
    "dDurationMs": 1270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "magnetic field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132209,
    "dDurationMs": 5521,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If that’s true then, why should we even expect there to be isolated magnetic charges - magnetic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 137730,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "monopoles?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138730,
    "dDurationMs": 3339,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, according the classical theory we shouldn’t."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142069,
    "dDurationMs": 5351,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The non-existence of magnetic monopoles is codified in the mathematics of electrodynamics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 147420,
    "dDurationMs": 5209,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In particular, Gauss’s law for magnetism,\none of the four Maxwell’s equations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152629,
    "dDurationMs": 4741,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It states that the divergence of a magnetic\nfield is zero."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 157370,
    "dDurationMs": 6099,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The divergence is just this mathy term for\nthe amount that a field points inward toward"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163469,
    "dDurationMs": 3951,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a sink or outward toward a source."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 167420,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Zero divergence means no source and no sink."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171140,
    "dDurationMs": 6219,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Magnetic field lines can form loops or head\nout toward infinity, but they never end."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177359,
    "dDurationMs": 2921,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "According to this law there are no magnetic\nmonopoles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 180280,
    "dDurationMs": 4829,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the other hand, Gauss’ law for electric\nfields tells us that the divergence of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 185109,
    "dDurationMs": 4300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electric field is not zero - it’s equal\nto the electric charge density."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 189409,
    "dDurationMs": 7350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That charge density is where the electric field lines can end - it forms their source or their sink."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196759,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So there are such things as isolated electric charges."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199279,
    "dDurationMs": 4201,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s take a quick gander at Maxwell’s\nequations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 203480,
    "dDurationMs": 3270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is them without any charges - electric or magnetic."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 206750,
    "dDurationMs": 3569,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "E is the electric field and B is the magnetic\nfield."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 210319,
    "dDurationMs": 4721,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s a near perfect symmetry between\nelectricity and magnetism which only gets"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 215040,
    "dDurationMs": 5130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "screwed up when you put in the electric charge - here in the form of charge density and current"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220170,
    "dDurationMs": 566,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "density."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220736,
    "dDurationMs": 4774,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You could also have symmetry between these equations if there was such a thing as magnetic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 225510,
    "dDurationMs": 1479,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "charge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 226989,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you add magnetic charges to these equations then you get a magnetic force that looks exactly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 231969,
    "dDurationMs": 2031,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like the electrostatic force."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 234000,
    "dDurationMs": 6069,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The physicist Murray Gell-Mann said that \"Everything not forbidden is compulsory.\""
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 240069,
    "dDurationMs": 5131,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "meaning that if the math of our physical theory allows it, then it exists in nature."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 245200,
    "dDurationMs": 4859,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s nothing in Maxwell’s equations\nthat really says magnetic monopoles can’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 250059,
    "dDurationMs": 5781,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exist except for the fact that James Clark\nMaxwell set the magnetic charge to zero because"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 255840,
    "dDurationMs": 2470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "he didn’t believe it existed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 258310,
    "dDurationMs": 3609,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in principle it could exist, and so could magnetic monopoles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261919,
    "dDurationMs": 2750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At least according to classical theory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 264669,
    "dDurationMs": 2261,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what about quantum mechanics?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 266930,
    "dDurationMs": 5630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When quantum theory first appeared it quickly revolutionized our understanding of electromagnetism"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 272560,
    "dDurationMs": 4829,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by explaining it in terms of quantum fields\nrather than charges and forces."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277389,
    "dDurationMs": 5941,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We talked previously how electromagnetism arose automatically from requiring that the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283330,
    "dDurationMs": 5589,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "equations of quantum mechanics had a particular symmetry - the measurements they predict are"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 288919,
    "dDurationMs": 5239,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "unaltered by changes in one simple property - the phase of the wavefunction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294158,
    "dDurationMs": 5862,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Electromagnetism pops into the equations as soon as we require this - but in that version"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 300020,
    "dDurationMs": 5660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of electromagnetism, the electric and magnetic fields are VERY different from each other,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305680,
    "dDurationMs": 3989,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and not at all interchangeable as they are\nin Maxwell’s equations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 309669,
    "dDurationMs": 6601,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In particular, the magnetic field emerging\nfrom the quantum theory must have zero divergence"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 316270,
    "dDurationMs": 6119,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- its field lines can never end - so it can’t\nhave its own charge, unlike the electric field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 322389,
    "dDurationMs": 5201,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So perhaps here we have our reason for the\napparent non-existence of magnetic monopoles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327590,
    "dDurationMs": 3560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Quantum mechanics, as the saying goes, forbids it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 331150,
    "dDurationMs": 2310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, not so fast."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 333460,
    "dDurationMs": 3820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Don’t underestimate the power of the obsessed physicist."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 337280,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The great Paul Dirac had a habit of discovering particles just by staring at the math."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 342360,
    "dDurationMs": 5649,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 1928 he predicted the existence of antimatter this way, as we’ve discussed in a previous"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 348009,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 349009,
    "dDurationMs": 5590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But then in 1931, just before his antimatter thing was verified, Dirac made another prediction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 354599,
    "dDurationMs": 3751,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- of the existence of magnetic monopoles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 358350,
    "dDurationMs": 2430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "His argument goes something like this."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 360780,
    "dDurationMs": 6330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you start with a dipole magnetic field,\nyou can approximate a monopole by moving the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 367110,
    "dDurationMs": 5690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ends far enough apart and somehow vanishing the connecting field lines."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372800,
    "dDurationMs": 1770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And there is a way to do that."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 374570,
    "dDurationMs": 5619,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you build a solenoid - just a coil carrying\nan electric current - you get a dipole field"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 380189,
    "dDurationMs": 3421,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "whose connecting field lines are constrained within the coil."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 383610,
    "dDurationMs": 5529,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So make the width of the coil much smaller\nthan the length, and it looks like two isolated"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 389139,
    "dDurationMs": 1541,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "magnetic charges."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 390680,
    "dDurationMs": 6329,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This construction is called the “Dirac string”, and Dirac’s argument is that if the string"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 397009,
    "dDurationMs": 5630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "part of the Dirac string is fundamentally\nundetectable, then magnetic monopoles can"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 402639,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exist."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 403639,
    "dDurationMs": 5451,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The second part of the argument is under what conditions that string is undetectable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 409090,
    "dDurationMs": 3419,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So magnetic fields affect charged particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 412509,
    "dDurationMs": 4750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In quantum mechanics, this works by shifting the phase of the particle’s wavefunction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 417259,
    "dDurationMs": 5201,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Imagine a charged particle - say an electron\n- passing by a Dirac string."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 422460,
    "dDurationMs": 4829,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To plot that trajectory you add up all possible paths of the electron, including paths to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 427289,
    "dDurationMs": 1960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the left and to the right at the string."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 429249,
    "dDurationMs": 5290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The presence of the string, with its magnetic\nfields, should introduce different phase shifts"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 434539,
    "dDurationMs": 4701,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "depending on which side of the string the electron passes - and that would actually"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 439240,
    "dDurationMs": 3060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have a noticeable effect on the path of the electron."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 442300,
    "dDurationMs": 2420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In other words, the string would be detectable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 444720,
    "dDurationMs": 4900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there’s one scenario where the string\ncan never be detected."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 449620,
    "dDurationMs": 4775,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The amount of the phase shift is proportional to the electric charge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 454395,
    "dDurationMs": 3354,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For the right value of that charge, the phase\nshift induced between the different sides"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 457749,
    "dDurationMs": 4711,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the string is exactly one wave cycle - which means no observable difference."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 462460,
    "dDurationMs": 5190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So for the Dirac string to be undetectable\nthen electric charge can only exist in integer"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 467650,
    "dDurationMs": 2741,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "multiples of that basic charge"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 470391,
    "dDurationMs": 5629,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is a very loose form of the argument - and you can get to it in different ways."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 476020,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the upshot is that the string connecting\nmonopoles is fundamentally unobservable, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 482100,
    "dDurationMs": 6599,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dirac argued that this makes it a mathematical figment, kind of like virtual particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 488699,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Reality should only be assigned to the monopoles themselves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 493139,
    "dDurationMs": 5481,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the one hand this was taken as a prediction of the quantization of electric charge - electric"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 498620,
    "dDurationMs": 6509,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "charge has to be discrete if there’s even\na single magnetic monopole in the entire universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 505129,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And of course we know that electric charge\nreally is quantized - it can only be integer"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 509289,
    "dDurationMs": 2201,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "multiples of the charge of the electron."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 511490,
    "dDurationMs": 3311,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or maybe of quarks - a third the electron charge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 514801,
    "dDurationMs": 4919,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But instead of taking this as a prediction\nof charge quantization, you can also flip"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 519720,
    "dDurationMs": 5740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it: magnetic monopoles are possible if electric charge is quantized."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 525460,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Charge turns out to be quantized, so quantum mechanics doesn’t actually forbid monopoles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 530700,
    "dDurationMs": 2890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s fast forward 40 more years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 533590,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the early 70s physicists had managed to\nexplain the weak nuclear force and unify it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 538670,
    "dDurationMs": 1840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with electromagnetism."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 540510,
    "dDurationMs": 4540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We talked about that before - about how the breaking of the symmetry of the Higgs field"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 545050,
    "dDurationMs": 3250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "separated the weak and electromagnetic forces."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 548300,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With that squared away, physicists were working to bring the strong nuclear force into the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 552380,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fold with the so-called Grand Unified Theories."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 556040,
    "dDurationMs": 3100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These involved slightly more complicated symmetry breakings."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 559140,
    "dDurationMs": 5910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well it turns out that magnetic monopoles\nare inevitable in all “GUTs”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 565050,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let me try to give you a sense of why - and\nwe have to talk about the Higgs field to do"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 572410,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 573410,
    "dDurationMs": 4100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ve done a couple of Higgs episodes before if you want to get all the nitty gritty."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 577510,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in electroweak theory, the Higgs field is a scalar field - it takes on a numerical value"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 583690,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "everywhere in the universe, but with no direction - it’s not a vector."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 588210,
    "dDurationMs": 4910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact it really takes on two complex values\neverywhere, and the interplay of those two"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 593120,
    "dDurationMs": 2800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“degrees of freedom” gives the Higgs its\npower."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 595920,
    "dDurationMs": 4470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the simplest grand unified theory, the\nHiggs field has three degrees of freedom instead"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 600390,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of two."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 601390,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means the field can sort of act like\na vector, even though it really isn’t one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 605310,
    "dDurationMs": 4820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It can have a little internal arrow that points\nin a particular direction - not pointing in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 610130,
    "dDurationMs": 3700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "physical space, but in the space of those\n3 degrees of freedom."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613830,
    "dDurationMs": 5210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now the laws of physics shouldn’t care about the relative internal values of the Higgs"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 619040,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field - what matters is the absolute length\nof that internal vector - not the direction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 623960,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s pointing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 624960,
    "dDurationMs": 4050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There should be no noticeable effect even\nif the direction of the Higgs field changes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 629010,
    "dDurationMs": 3140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "smoothly across space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 632150,
    "dDurationMs": 1880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Except in one very special case."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 634030,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If the direction of the Higgs field varies\nsmoothly from one point to the next, it can"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 638990,
    "dDurationMs": 4550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "still have these sorts of knots - places where the field arrows all point away from that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 643540,
    "dDurationMs": 4220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "point - in what was called a hedgehog configuration."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 647760,
    "dDurationMs": 6530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These are topological discontinuities - points that can’t be removed by a smooth defomation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 654290,
    "dDurationMs": 1400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 655690,
    "dDurationMs": 5950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And it turns out these knots in the Higgs\nfield in GUT theories behave as massive particles"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 661640,
    "dDurationMs": 3430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with magnetic charge - magnetic monopoles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 665070,
    "dDurationMs": 6790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The hedgehog solution was figured out in 1974, simultaneously by Gerard t’Hooft and Alexander"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 671860,
    "dDurationMs": 1040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Polyakov."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 672900,
    "dDurationMs": 5810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It turns out that GUT theories generically\npredict these magnetic monopoles, and that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 678710,
    "dDurationMs": 7220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they should be A) very massive, and B) should form spontaneously in extremely high-energy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 685930,
    "dDurationMs": 3370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "environments like in the very early universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 689300,
    "dDurationMs": 7130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The fact that magnetic monopoles should exist in these theories was both exciting and problematic."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696430,
    "dDurationMs": 6150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "GUTs predict that monopoles should be produced in enormous numbers in the very early universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 702580,
    "dDurationMs": 3610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- as abundantly as protons and electrons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 706190,
    "dDurationMs": 1680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So where are they all?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 707870,
    "dDurationMs": 4230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They should also be very massive - quadrillions of times the mass of the proton - and so should"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 712100,
    "dDurationMs": 2480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have quickly recollapsed the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 714580,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This conflict with reality might have ruled out both monopoles and the grand unified theories that predict them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 722180,
    "dDurationMs": 7490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Except that both of these ideas are saved by yet another speculative idea - cosmic inflation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 729670,
    "dDurationMs": 5590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Many physicists think that a period of prodigious exponential growth kicked off the expansion"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 735260,
    "dDurationMs": 1450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of our universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 736710,
    "dDurationMs": 4290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This should have happened after the production of magnetic monopoles, and so should have"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 741000,
    "dDurationMs": 5770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thrown these things far apart that there may be very few remaining in our entire observable"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 746770,
    "dDurationMs": 1940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 748710,
    "dDurationMs": 2780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That would be a bummer for our hopes of detecting these things."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 751490,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that hasn’t stopped physicists from\ntrying."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 754370,
    "dDurationMs": 2560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are various approaches."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 756930,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you have one of these magnetic monopoles in your lab it wouldn’t be too hard to spot"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 761730,
    "dDurationMs": 6530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- for example a monopole would excite an electric current if passed through a conducting coil."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 768260,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Back in 1982, physicist Blas Cabrera Navarro set up a superconducting coil in his Stanford"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 774820,
    "dDurationMs": 5420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lab and managed to detect what looked like\na monopole with the same charge predicted"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 780240,
    "dDurationMs": 1930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by Paul Dirac."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 782170,
    "dDurationMs": 4390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But no one ever saw such a thing ever again."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 786560,
    "dDurationMs": 4190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That includes at the Large Hadron Collider,\nwhere a couple of different experiments have"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 790750,
    "dDurationMs": 2490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "failed to spot monopoles created in the collider."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 793240,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That's not so surprising given that the\nLHC reaches energies about 100 billion times"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 799000,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lower than is needed to produce the monopoles predicted by grand unified theories."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 804310,
    "dDurationMs": 4529,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "People also look for magnetic monopoles coming from space - typically using cosmic ray observatories"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 808839,
    "dDurationMs": 4331,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- or contributing to the Earth’s magnetic\nfield - and in a number of other ways."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 813170,
    "dDurationMs": 4450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But again, no convincing evidence as of\nyet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 817620,
    "dDurationMs": 4900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We have been hunting for magnetic monopoles for longer than just about any particle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 822520,
    "dDurationMs": 4820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Their discovery would mean cracking open the grand unified theories and revealing mysteries"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 827340,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "far beyond."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 828340,
    "dDurationMs": 7070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And so many of us remain obsessed with this elusive beast, and convinced of its inevitability"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 835410,
    "dDurationMs": 3650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "according to the symmetries of space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 839060,
    "dDurationMs": 5250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you've joined us on Patreon, I can't emphasize enough how helpful your support is in keeping"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 844310,
    "dDurationMs": 1620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this show going."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 845930,
    "dDurationMs": 4830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You don't have to do it - you could keep watching for free anyway - but that makes it all the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 850760,
    "dDurationMs": 2320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more awesome of you."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 853080,
    "dDurationMs": 5410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today I want to give a huge shoutout to our Big Bang supporter Kyle Bulloch."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 858490,
    "dDurationMs": 5370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Kyle, we are taught by Paul Dirac that if\nthere's even a single magnetic monopole in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 863860,
    "dDurationMs": 4490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the entire universe then electric charge must be quantized."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 868350,
    "dDurationMs": 5350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We don't know where that single magnetic monopole would be, but it's rare and special and so"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 873700,
    "dDurationMs": 2490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we decided to name it after you."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 876190,
    "dDurationMs": 6490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So now Kyle, the magnetic monopole Kyle - is doubly important - sure, it ensures the quantization"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 882680,
    "dDurationMs": 4930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of all charge, but it also serves as a testament to the generosity of Kyle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 887610,
    "dDurationMs": 2230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Of Kyle the human."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 889840,
    "dDurationMs": 5260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Kyle, you have our thanks and now you also have a monopole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 895100,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Last week we talked about how quantum spin leads to the universe as we know it - for"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 899420,
    "dDurationMs": 5370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "example all the structure of solids, via the\nPauli exclusion principle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 904790,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "4fmagnet points out a potentially misleading point in that episdoe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 908790,
    "dDurationMs": 5870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We represented very distinctly separated electron energy levels in our explanation of how fermion’s can’t occupy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 914660,
    "dDurationMs": 2030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "identical quantum states."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 916690,
    "dDurationMs": 5620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "4fmagnet notes that energy levels in atoms\ncan actually hold 2 electrons, not one, because"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 922310,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s possible to have two electrons at the\nsame energy but different quantum states by"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 927410,
    "dDurationMs": 2070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "allowing for opposite spin orientations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 929480,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That's a fair call - if I was being more careful I would have said that we were trying to represent"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 935080,
    "dDurationMs": 4410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "separate quantum states, not energy levels\nin an atom."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 939490,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "David points out that we don’t need to keep\ntalking about spin as this incomprehensible"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 944330,
    "dDurationMs": 5410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum property that has no intuitive analogy - reminding us that Hans Ohanian, in his book"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 949740,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“what is spin?”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 950740,
    "dDurationMs": 4870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "showed that spin can be described as a circular charge current in the Dirac field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 955610,
    "dDurationMs": 5110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We actually did mention this result in our episode on what is spin."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 960720,
    "dDurationMs": 6530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And it may be that people should talk about this result more - Ohanian’s calculation is not"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 967250,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "generally accepted as “true”, so I’m\nnot sure we can be so confident in it,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 972250,
    "dDurationMs": 3830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "although as far as I know no-one has actually disputed the math."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 976080,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Still, it would be nice to have a physical\npicture of spin that doesn’t involve me"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 981040,
    "dDurationMs": 1380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "taking off my belt."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 982420,
    "dDurationMs": 4790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Flosi Lyons asks what happens when the degeneracy pressure - the structural support against"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 987210,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collapse produced by the Pauli exclusion principle - is overcome."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 992170,
    "dDurationMs": 4830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, if a collapsing stellar core\nis massive enough it smashes through the degeneracy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 997000,
    "dDurationMs": 5810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pressure that supports white dwarf stars and instead produces a black hole or neutron star."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1002810,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So the answer is that the Pauli exclusion\nprinciple is never violated."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1009010,
    "dDurationMs": 2590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fermions never occupy the same quantum states."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1011600,
    "dDurationMs": 4730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When degeneracy pressure is broken it’s\nbecause new space in quantum states opens"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1016330,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1017330,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the case of the collapsing star, densities\nand energies become high enough for electrons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1022050,
    "dDurationMs": 3820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be captured by protons, converting them\nto neutrons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1025870,
    "dDurationMs": 5180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So the loss of electrons reduces the degeneracy\npressure, allowing gravitational collapse"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1031050,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to continue."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1032050,
    "dDurationMs": 6790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Tom Rostrom asks whether any of this spin\nstatistics stuff explains why USB plugs need"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1038840,
    "dDurationMs": 5770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be turned 540° to return to the correct\norientation?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1044610,
    "dDurationMs": 1870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fantastic question, Tim."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1046480,
    "dDurationMs": 1620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The answer is no."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1048100,
    "dDurationMs": 6630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "USB plugs have spin 2/3s - a 360 degree rotation\ngets you 2/3 of the way around."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1054730,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They’re neither fermions nor bosons, so\nway outside the standard model."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1060910,
    "dDurationMs": 7380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Also, remember that you can undo fermion rotations by translation of the ends - for USB cables"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1068290,
    "dDurationMs": 5020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its the opposite- twists approach infinity\nas you move the ends around, especially in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1073310,
    "dDurationMs": 3050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the presence of other USB cables."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1076360,
    "dDurationMs": 5450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Physicists hope to understand these things\nsomeday, perhaps with twistor or string theory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1081810,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or maybe you could just use USBC - they’re spin-2 bosons like gravitons, so easily understood with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1088610,
    "dDurationMs": 2158,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a basic theory of quantum gravity."
    } ]
  } ]
}
